  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nThrough This Morning: Uncertain Severe Storm Chances\n\nMultiple clusters of showers and storms continue to develop \nfrom southeastern Manitoba to north central Wisconsin at the \ntime of this writing. Even though the environment is conducive \nfor severe storms to form with plenty of instability and shear, \nthe upper level ridge is keeping the convection mostly \nsuppressed so far throughout this event. Mid level capping \ncontinues to win out even though a few storms so far this \nevening in northeastern Minnesota have shown signs of tapping \ninto the better environment, but they have not lasted long. \nUpper level lift, which is currently located over northeastern \nND into Manitoba, should continue to round the ridge and develop\nstorms over northern Minnesota. Those storms should move \nsoutheast into Wisconsin going into this morning.\n\nThere continues to be tremendous uncertainty regarding timing, \nlocation, and severity of storms tonight into Monday morning. \nThe hi-res forecast models continue to have no run-to-run \nconsistency and all offer differing solutions. Based on recent \nobservational trends and satellite interpretation, the current \nexpectation is leaning towards a later start to any potential \nsevere storms, potentially not impacting the forecast area until\nsunrise or later on this morning. If that scenario ends up \npanning out, the potential is there for a damaging wind gust \nevent across portions of Wisconsin and further east, especially \nif any sort of daytime heating can occur before storms reach the\nregion leading to lesser CIN. Large hail would also be possible\nbut a lesser concern as the storms are expected to form along a\nline. Another potential scenario is that the storms or line of \nstorms completely misses the FA to the northeast and east if the\naxis of the ridge is too far northeast across the area, which \nwould direct storms around the CWA. There are even further \nsolutions that suggest the storms that are currently ongoing \nwill be able to form into severe storms in the next few hours. \nThat scenario currently has the lowest confidence in panning out\nbased on observational trends with the other two scenarios \nhaving equal confidence in occurring, even though that \nconfidence remains very low right now.\n\nAdditional development may occur as the shortwave along with a \nsurface boundary move across the region this morning. Model \nguidance shows the front moving across during the morning hours \nand where ever the front interacts with a potential line of \nstorms or any outflow boundaries, that could be enough to \nquickly generate storms. Where or if any interaction occurs \nremains very low in confidence. To summarize this all, \nconfidence in any severe storms and their timing or location, is\nvery low but the environment will remain highly conducive for \nsevere storm development through this morning.\n\nRest of Today: Dangerous Heat and Additional Storm Chances\n\nConfidence in the dangerous heat has also decreased due to the \nlater push of potential storms along with cloud cover. Plentiful\nsurface moisture is expected to remain in place to lead to high\nheat index values if sufficient daytime heating can occur, \nwhich could lead to heat index values of 100 or higher. The \npotential for morning fog combined with cloud cover and \npotential convection has led to uncertainty in the highest \nafternoon temperatures at this time. The previous heat headlines\nremain in place given the high potential still for heat index \nvalues to reach or exceed 100 across most of the area. With how \nmuch moisture is in place, a few isolated afternoon storms may \ntry to form but confidence in that remains quite low.\n\nTuesday into Next Weekend: Warm Temperatures and Storm Chances\n\nThe ridge shifts westward just enough and northwest flow \nremains in place mid week. Both of these will mean temperatures \ncool down marginally, into the mid to upper 80s along with lower\nheat index values. The potential for heat index values to reach\nor exceed the triple digit mark is low Tuesday and beyond. With\nthis flow pattern, ensemble guidance has low precipitation \nchances through midweek with better chances as we head towards \nthe weekend. The ridge is expected to become suppressed far \nenough south for stronger troughs to push across the Northern \nPlains into the Midwest which should trigger a few rounds of \nrain potential at the end of this week into the weekend.",
